    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Alodia was the last of the three Nubian kingdoms to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>: in 580 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Longinus baptized the king, his family and the nobility.</li><li>In the 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> and al-Aswani found a large, polyethnic kingdom more powerful than Makuria, with a capital of fine buildings and churches full of gold.</li><li>Succession was matrilineal, passing to the son of the king's sister, and al-Aswani wrote that the king could enslave any subject at will.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>As late as 1930, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamaj">Hamaj</a> villagers in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira">Gezira</a> would swear by 'Soba the home of my grandfathers and grandmothers which can make the stone float and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> ball sink'.</p></aside>
    </section>

